{"author":{"id":"694fb8161bd8bf91df2a28db7c649e48f8c5fa47ad3ad0234607d35f0482801a"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1790415816,"nonce":"JMK3QTyyFVCvYkpTDtoJlRLlAMA=","title":"Per-call bookkeeping in VM::call_constructor_sync","message":"Profile from the caribou session (NUC i5-1250P, pinned to P-cores, PEBS cpu_core/instructions/upp), `cargo bench -p caribou-interop --bench interop -- --only construct --column 2` (Haxe-\u003eWren construct): one construct is ~2000 instructions (~120 ns); call_constructor_sync is ~9% of them.\n\nWhat runs on every call (src/runtime/vm.rs, call_constructor_sync_impl, checked at f7cf6c1):\n- engine.func_module(func_id) and module_vars_for(func_id): two lookups per call, fixed per function.\n- (*fiber).mir_frames.iter().any(...): a scan of the fiber's interpreter frames on every call.\n- roots push / extend_from_slice, then a copy from the roots into jit_args.\n- A __memmove attributed to the function.\n\nCandidates: cache the module context per function (or on the closure); skip the frame scan when the function has no interpreter frames (a per-function count); build the argument array once.\n\nAllocation: caribou reports the instance and its fields as two allocations (alloc_plain, then ObjInstance.fields with fields_owned), about half the allocator samples in the construct. wren_lift's own Immix allocator already places fields inline (gc_immix test instance_fields_are_inline), so the second allocation comes from the caribou heap's alloc_instance through the runtime seam; worth checking there or giving the seam an inline-fields contract.","files":null}]}